1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Romanian shepherds saw — a farm
in the stars.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
In 1896, Ion Otescu sent a star chart to
village teachers all over Romania.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
The question: what are the stars called?

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
In summer, farmers told time by them.
Otherwise, only shepherds kept watching.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,000
High in the north, Capella is Capra, the
she-goat. Three faint stars beside her
are her kids.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:42,000
South: the brightest star, Sirius, sits
on Dulăul, the big dog that guards the
sheep. Procyon is a little dog.

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
West: the Pleiades are a hen with her
chicks.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
The Hyades: a herd of boars. Aldebaran:
“the pigs’ bright star” — some say “the
Alarm Clock.”

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
And the shepherd himself? Vega. The four
faint stars beside him are his sheep.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
This time of year, he rises only late at
night, in the northeast.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:15,000
So the sky has a well, too: the Great
Square of Pegasus is Puțul, the well.
Andromeda is the water trough.

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
After dark: Sirius in the south, the
she-goat high overhead. Before dawn, the
Shepherd is high in the east.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Tukano: the Amazon’s rain calendar in
the stars.

14
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

